<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Druze</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Druzes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Druze are an Arabic-speaking religious community whose faith arose from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the early 11th century, around the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Hakim. Closed to converts since the mid-11th century, they live mainly in the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and northern Israel. Druze lords dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> under the Ma'n <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, and the community led the Syrian revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1925.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/druze.jpg" alt="Druze" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dr. Avishai Teicher · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PikiWiki_Israel_45144_Nabi_Shuayb.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
